Subject: B-cache after upgraded cpu's
Date: 1999/10/30
Message-ID: <01bf22e2$8044a980$bd2a883e@compaq>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 542435008
X-Complaints-To: abuse@theplanet.net
X-Trace: news5.svr.pol.co.uk 941296473 6716 62.136.42.189 (30 Oct 1999 15:14:33 GMT)
Organization: Customer of Planet Online
NNTP-Posting-Date: 30 Oct 1999 15:14:33 GMT
Newsgroups: comp.sys.dec

I have upgraded the CPU's on several Alpha 1000's from 4/266 to 5/300.
Since the upgrades the incidents of b-cache errors being clocked has
increased dramatically. I carried out the cpu upgrade from the V5.3 CD  and
later using the V5.4 CD supplied with the CPU's. Following the instructions
to the letter including ESD protection.

I have been told this could be due to the memory being of mixed makes, e.i.
Original DEC simms in bank 1 supplied with the servers when new. Other
banks have Kingston simms.  The error correction simms have been removed
now that the servers are EV5 and are not required.  

Allegedly, the Kingston simms may be not as tight on speed spec as the DEC
simms. The DEC simms are 70 ns and tin contacts. The Kingston are 60ns gold
edge. I know about the problems mixing different metals on edge connection
causing reaction but our purchaser looks at price and says we'll live with
it by clean and reseat when a problem occurs.

The main concern is the dramatic increase in incidents. Faster CPU = more
frequent memory access = more visible errors, is my theory.......ANY IDEAS
or COMMENTS welcome.?????

Colin.





