news:7v4v3q$im4$1@lead.zk3.dec.com...
>
> The sysconfig(8) man page may help, as well as the sysconfigtab(4) man
> page.  The subsystem name is 'proc'.
>
> # sysconfig -q proc | sort # I like sorted output   8-)
> [...]
> max-per-proc-address-space = 1073741824
> max-per-proc-data-size = 1073741824
> max-per-proc-stack-size = 33554432
> [...]
> #
>
> You will want to add a 'proc' stanza to the file /etc/sysconfigtab (or
> add to the one already present):
>
> proc:
> max-per-proc-stack-size = 104857600
>
> and reboot the system.  Some subsystems' attributes allow 'change on
> the fly'; this is not one of them:
>
> # sysconfig -r proc max-per-proc-stack-size=104857600
> max-per-proc-stack-size: attribute does not allow this operation
> #
>
> I hope this helps,
