SKC Previews "ICUS" San Diego

(c) 1999 by Terry Shannon, Consultant and Publisher, Shannon Knows Compaq

It's less than a month until the first Integrated Compaq User Symposium
(ICUS) will be held in sunny San Diego, CA.  Since this will be the final
twice-yearly instantiation of The IT Event Formerly Known as DECUS, and
since the next convocation of Compaq users won't take place until October
2000, the San Diego Convention Center is the place to be from November 6
through 11, 1999. SKC will be on hand to participate in our 31st consecutive
symposium, and we highly recommend this event to our constituency. Presented
herewith in our traditional Top Ten format is a preview of next month's
multi-platform, multi-group, and multi-cultural "ICUS" festivities.

10) Find out why WildFire redefines "Hot Iron." A variety of symposium
sessions will shed light on Compaq's forthcoming AlphaServer GS80/GS160/
GS320 WildFire enterprise server lineup. Learn why WildFire will eclipse
StarFire and other competitive high-end offerings. Get detailed WildFire
information in a PID-free environment, and spend some quality time with the
new hardware during live product demos.

9) Live from San Diego, it's Mike Capellas! A sudden decision to pursue new
career opportunities forced former Compaq CEO Eckhard Pfeiffer to cancel his
keynote address at the June 1999 DECUS US Chapter Symposium in Providence,
RI. Newly anointed President and CEO Michael D. Capellas will be on hand to
kick off the San Diego symposium with a keynote presentation at 8:30 a.m. on
Monday November 8. Here's your opportunity to hear the Chief Compaq-tian
articulate his strategy for putting Team Houston back on track.

8) Be 'Tru' to your Unix. The scuttling of the Bravo initiative means that
Tru64 UNIX won't be coming to an IA-64 system near you, but the Q remains
true to Tru64. In recognition of this fact-and to prove that ICUS isn't just
for OpenVMS any more-the symposium will feature a first-ever Unix Day on
Tuesday November 9. The day-long "all Unix, all the time" session stream is
open to all four-day symposium registrants; Unix devotees can attend Unix
Day only for a special price of $199. For an even heavier Unix fix, why not
take advantage of one or two of the six full-day Unix Presymposium Seminars,
and the more than 25 hours of Unix sessions throughout the week?

7) Dazed and confused by storage? Let the Q's spin doctors straighten you
out at National Storage Day on Wednesday November 10. Colocated with the
symposium, this day-long seminar will bring you up to date with current and
future StorageWorks products and strategies. (More information is at www.
compaq.com/storageworks; call 1-800-457-8211 to register.)

6) DECUS, ICUS, what-us? The times, they are a-changing, and DECUS must
change with the times. Help DECUS march into the new millennium as part of a
new Compaq user society. As a DECUS member, you can help shape the new
society by attending the US Chapter Open Board of Directors meeting and by
chatting one-on-one with US Chapter Board members including SKC's publisher.
DECUS leaders from international chapters will be on hand in San Diego as
well, affording you the opportunity to discuss global user group issues with
DECUS delegates from Australia, Europe, and South America.

5) Remedy your OpenVMS ills at the Free Clinic. Once again, OpenVMS
Engineering will host its popular evening clinic sessions. Scheduled for 6
p.m.on Monday and Wednesday, the clinics cover a variety of OpenVMS-centric
issues including OS installation, the Galaxy Software Architecture, RMS file
tuning, and device drivers. Novice OpenVMS system managers requiring
intensive therapy can opt to spend all four symposium days attending the
DallasFort Worth Compaq User Group's OpenVMS Class Series. Those who
complete the DFWCUG's treatment regimen will be well equipped to deal with
the myriad challenges and opportunities of OpenVMS system administration.

4) Do you view Windows 'through a glass, darkly'? Go off to see the Wizards!
When it comes to prowess and prestidigitation in the Windows realm, Compaq's
NT Wizards rule. The Q's NT-savvy techies will demystify NT in 23
Wizard-sponsored sessions. All told, ICUS will feature seven NT-related
Presymposium Seminars and over 35 hours of NT-related sessions during the
week.

3) Got a bone to pick with the Q? Care to share your innermost thoughts on
the AlphaNT debacle with senior Compaq executives? The Tuesday evening
Compaq Listens panel provides an opportunity to get things off your chest,
make yourself heard, or offer your suggestions in a target-rich environment
populated by Compaq VPs and managers. As usual, VP of Alpha Technology Jesse
Lipcon will be your host for this entertaining and insightful session.

2) Want a second opinion on Compaq's products and strategic directions? Of
course you do! Get a sanity check and arm yourself with the latest rumours,
code-names, and probing questions for the Compaq Listens panel by attending
SKC's ever-popular Compaq's Strategic Directions session. This 90-minute
presentation takes place at 4:30 p.m.on Tuesday, immediately before the
Compaq Listens inquisition.

1) And the Number One reason to attend the San Diego symposium? San Diego is
known for boot camp, but SKC believes training should be fun. And we
guarantee ICUS Week at the San Diego Convention Center will be more fun than
Hell Week at the US Navy SEAL training facility on nearby Coronado Island!

There you have 'em: ten really compelling reasons to attend the Integrated
Compaq Users Symposium in San Diego. Time is running short, so be sure to
call 1-800-DECUS55 or visit http:// www.decus.org Real Soon Now to register
for the event. Early registration has been extended, so you can still
qualify for the "early bird" symposium registration fee of $1395. Additional
pricing information, and details on accommodation, can be found at the
website. SKC hopes to see you in San Diego!


--
Terry C. Shannon
Consultant and Publisher, Shannon Knows Compaq
shannon@world.std.com
http://www.acersoft.com





