There may be some confusion here.  Currently for VMS you have a base license and
than add user licenses.  The base license is for the version of the OS at the
time
of purchase and the warranty period.  Once the warranty expires, unless you have
a software support/maintenance contract, you are limited to the version at that
time.  If you want a later version, it is about the same as buying a new base
license, with possibly some discount.  Don't know if it is Ok to go on support
and then use the newest version.  I'd think so, since they don't support older
versions.  Without looking at legalities, the PAK you have with the original
license will work with all future versions.

For the VAXstation 4000 VLC I'm not sure what the story is.  At one time DEC
bundled the VMS license with the hardware, and the concept of a base license
as it exists today wasn't quite the same.  Regardless, a software support
contract
would allow you to constantly use the latest OS version.  There are various
flavors
of these, from the simple "right to new version" to quarterly CD-ROM
distributions
with telephone support.  I subscribe to the cheapest, and purchase media as
required.
I'm not going to do the research, you're as capable as I am, but you should be
able
to get a new base VMS license for under $2k, way under.  Or, you may qualify for
the hobbiest program.

Dave

