licenses" can only be used on a single CPU. Concurrent licenses can also
transfer from one system to another ( a natural side effect of allowing
them to be shared ), whereas traditional licenses are only good for 
the CPU for which they are originally purchased.

     If one has any intention of clustering, or upgrading the system, I'd
recommend a serious look at concurrent licenses rather than traditional.
Remember, when you dump that system in a few years because it's obsolete
any money you've invested in a base or traditional license is forfeit.

  (disclaimer: all the above is just my understanding of the rules, don't
blame me if Compaq disagrees )

