In article <3815230F.26E2574@connect.net.au>,
Greg Thomas  <greg_t@connect.net.au> wrote:
>I'm new to VAXen.
>
>But I've got myself a 3100 m76 with 32MB RAM that I'm intending to put
>NetBSD onto. Can someone tell me roughly how fast these machines are (PC
>processing terms would be a good start) and what they are especially
>good for. My understanding so far is that they are pretty weak processor
>wise, but very good for some specialised form of I/O or multi user or
>something. Am I on the right track? Can someone quantify the speed for
>me?

They're quite nice little boxes.  Speedwise it's 7.6 VUPS or 7.6 times
the processing power of the original VAX 11/780.  This also translates to
7.6 MIPS worth of processing power.

In PC terms... well running the Dhrystone benchmark under NetBSD/i386 on
my 386DX/40 I get around 22,000 Dhry/s.  On my VAXstation 3100/M76 I get
around 18,500 Dhry/s.  This would place the M76 in the ballpark of a
386DX/33.  The VAX does have really nice floating point support though, so
it'd be more like a 386DX/33 with an 80387.

Beyond that, the VAX, IMHO, is a whole lot nicer architecture than the
Intel.  It's a clean design that aged very gracefully as its
implementation progressed.  Its instruction set is one of richest of all
the 32bit processors.  And being *very* much a CISC design, the code
density is great.  You can do a whole lot more with 32Megs of RAM on a VAX
than you can with an equivalent amount of RAM on the 32bit MIPS or Sparc
processors, or even the 32bit Intel processors.  The code density becomes
apparent when you start looking at sizes of the various NetBSD ports.  If
I'm not mistaken, the NetBSD/vax distribution is the tightest of all of
them. 

>Would it make a good MUD server?

Sure.  I'm not sure how much MUD servers have evolved since the early
1990s, but I know of some which were run on far more modest hardware than
what the VAXstation 3100/M76 has to offer.

-brian.
