Similarly, the Pathworks/Advanced server includes no client licenses.
It lays out like this (and the folks in OpenVMS should really be ashamed 
that it does).  Out of the box this is what the following OS's offer:

OpenVMS     Tru64           WNT
1           infinite        NA               concurrent interactive sessions
no          yes (NIS/NFS)   yes (SMB,PDC)    clustering (native variant)
no          no              >=10 connections file serving to Windows
desupported no              yes              file serving to Mac
yes         yes             no               file serving/reading to Unix

The story is somewhat better than it appears for Tru64 because you can load
Samba for free, and Unix Samba is in much better shape than is VMS Samba.
We use Samba on Linux as a PDC and it works ok for our needs, probably it
would work as well on Tru64.  But there are no PDC bits in the VMS variant.

The only NO in the WNT list can be remedied easily enough as NFS is
available from several vendors.

Clustering can be had for OpenVMS but it will cost more than the base 
machine.  File/print service to Windows can be purchased (client licenses
for Advanced Server) but they will cost 5-10X more than WNT (and infinitely
more than Samba) and performance is reportedly terrible compared to either.
There are no supported methods for making VMS machines talk to Macs or
their appletalk printers anymore.  You can't get that for love or money. 
However, if you've got existing PW/Mac licenses you can use it until some
hardware/ software release breaks it.  (Not something I would try in an 
industrial setting, as when PW/Mac goes down it usually takes the whole 
system with it.)

Bottom line - besides costing more than WNT (Intel only now) and Tru64
(same hardware) the base OpenVMS machine is severely hobbled in that it: 

1.  won't cluster with other VMS machine (negating the administrative advantages)
2.  won't interact with Windows machines (in their lingua franca)
3.  can't talk to Macintosh machines     (ditto)
4.  won't allow any interactive users

With properties like those perhaps OpenVMS marketing should target not only
the very high end but the very low as well.  It makes VMS sound more like
an embedded controller than a modern general purpose OS. 

