andrewg@no.spam.please.bsw.co.za (Andrew Gaylard) writes:

> Is there anything which will show the exact locations of these
> libraries, a la ldd?  Is there a tool which can show which libraries a
> running process is using a la Sun's pldd, so I don't have to stop it and
> restart it?

I haven't come across a way to get a run-time library list without
running the program.  If you an stand that, then you can do this:
	env _RLD_ARGS=-v <run-program>

e.g.:

% env _RLD_ARGS=-v ls
30641:/usr/bin/ls: /sbin/loader: _RLD_ARGS = "-v"
30641:/usr/bin/ls: /sbin/loader: _RLD_ROOT = /
30641:/usr/bin/ls: /sbin/loader: mapped Main at 0x120000000
30641:/usr/bin/ls: /sbin/loader: mapped /usr/shlib/libc.so at 0x3ff80080000
30641:/usr/bin/ls: /sbin/loader: obj /usr/shlib/libc.so CHECKSUM CHANGED
30641:/usr/bin/ls: /sbin/loader: Loader heap at 0x3ffc0008290
<prints out directory listing here>


As for getting a list of libraries in use by a running process, you
might try the lsof package (start at
ftp://vic.cc.purdue.edu/pub/tools/unix/lsof/)
It can identify open and mapped files, which should include the shared
libraries.
--
John Kohl <jtk@rational.com>
