Hi, Michael,

The likely cause of the hang in doing NFS mounts is nfs entries in
/etc/fstab.  CTRL/C does terminate the startup script
(/sbin/rc3.d/S??nfsmount, a symbolic link to /sbin/init.d/nfsmount)
(             ^^20 on my system )
and allow the boot to multi-user to continue.

To get to single user mode, you want to get to the SRM console prompt
(>>> or P00>>>, depends on your box).  Press the RESET button (or,
cycle the power).  It sounds like your system will attempt to
auto-boot; repeatedly pressing CTRL/C and/or CTRL/P will terminate the
console firmware boot process and give you the console prompt.  Issue
the command

>>> boot -fl "s"

to set the "single-user-mode" flag when booting the default boot file
(usually vmunix, add the -fl "filename" flag to specify another kernel,
such as genvmunix), and the default boot device.  The command

>>> show boot*
will show the boot variables known to the console firmware.

The system will boot to single user mode (should even say so), with the
root filesystem mounted readonly and the root command prompt.  Issue

# bcheckrc

to mount the root filesystem read/write, and (fsck, if UFS, and) mount
the local filesystems (usr and var).  You may now use your favorite
UNIX editor to edit the /etc/fstab nfs entries.  You could delete them,
or just change the how-mount option (ro or rw) to xx; they will be
skipped at nfsmount time.

You may also want to run /usr/sbin/setup to configure your system for
networking, NIS, NTP, mail and other services before terminating the
single-user command shell (CTRL/D) and continuing to multi-user mode.

I hope this helps you; let me know.

Best regards,
=Aaron
