  said, no representation of the number of other random pieces of horse 
  anatomy "present" and contributing here in the newsgroups will be made. :-)

:For context, here is the pack of bare-faced lies and screw-you tactics
:that made me not buy a 600 MHz LX last year.  Verbatim:
:
:------------------------------------------------------------------------------
:The LX motherboard with a full-function 21164 (not the NT part), an
:SRM console firmware load, and a Digital UNIX license and CD will
:certainly run Digital UNIX.  However this is not a complete answer to
:your question.
:
:The bundle of the LX motherboard with the P8 processor is intended to
:be a cost effective Windows NT solution kit.  The P8 processor is
:technically different from the full-function UNIX 21164s in that it
:does not support a specific memory management mode that UNIX (and VMS)
:requires and Windows NT does not.  From time to time, since the full
:function part is a full superset of the NT part, we will mark full
:function parts as P8s to fulfill demand
..

  There is nothing in the included message that appears to be incorrect 
  or even misleading.  With a view to the then-DIGITAL-internal discussions 
  and the Alpha microprocessor that was targeted for use in that timeframe, 
  that message is a whole lot closer to one potential reality than folks
  apparently think it was.  Specific Alpha microprocessors can be configured 
  to run only with the superpage mapping used by Windows NT.  (I do not
  know if any of these Alpha microprocessors ever shipped.)

  For better or worse and whether or not folks might agree with it, what 
  was posted certainly appears very close to my own understanding of the 
  then-current (circa 1996?) marketing plan for Alpha systems and Alpha
  widgets in the Windows NT space.

  At this time, the plan is to not restrict the hardware, and to reduce
  the number of unnecessary system variations.

