Eddie,

Two things to try:

1. Replace the C2032 3v Lithium battery on the motherboard, then enter
the system CMOS setup and auto-detect the hard drive.  Finally, save
system settings and reboot.  This works if the battery has crapped
out, which they do after a while.  If that fails...

1. Prepare a bootable Windows 95 floppy with the SYS command on it.
(You may need to have access to somebody else's system to do so.)
Boot from floppy, execute the command "SYS C:" from the DOS command
prompt.  Without the quotes, of course.  This works if the boot block
on the hard drive has gotten mangled by some rogue (virus?) software
or a hardware malfunction.

Otherwise, reinstall Windows 95.  or is it 98?  ... Ben Myers

