In article <s11erclv5n684@corp.supernews.com>, Eddie <Cellysrus@aol.com> writes:
:When I power up my Digital Celebris GL 5166 -it goes thru its checks and  
:finally reads "operating system not found". I am not able to get into 
:system at all. I've rebooted several times but no luck. A friend suggested 
:it could be the motherboard. Before this happened, I compressed my cdrive 
:abt a couple of days before this all started.  Any suggestions - I need 
:Help!!!

  The PC design and the "standard" PC BIOS and the standard Microsoft Windows 
  bootstrap processing combine to produce a startup environment that is 
  -- based on my experience with VAX and Alpha consoles -- rather primitive
  and correspondingly rather difficult to control and to debug.  (I most 
  miss the ability to directly specify an alternate system "root" and/or 
  an alternate target disk for the bootstrap.  But that is beyond the
  standard feature set that is expected in the PC space.)  All that said, 
  more than a few times I've seen this "operating system not found error" 
  error due to a stomped-on operating system, and I've also seen it arise 
  from hard disk and/or BIOS problems, and it would not surprise me that 
  it can also be caused by bad hardware.

  The usual initial answer for this class of question is the tried-and-true
  "try a reinstallation of the Microsoft operating system"...  This is not
  an approach I prefer to use (for many reasons), but an fdisk pass followed 
  by a full reinstallation is one of the typical ways that one can initially 
  try to resolve these sorts of bootstrap problems.

 --------------------------- pure personal opinion ---------------------------
   Hoff (Stephen) Hoffman   OpenVMS Engineering   hoffman#xdelta.zko.dec.com




