In article <rz7d7uacfyp.fsf@corton.inria.fr>, 
   Robert Harley <harley@corton.inria.fr> writes:
> 
> Today you can say "sure you can run VMS on a DS10" and tomorrow
> somebody else will say "technically you can, but you're not allowed".
> 

    You can buy DS10 packages with VMS or Unix today. This indicates
that the DS10 is a supported processor for VMS/Unix. Therefore if you were
to buy one of these "Linux" DS10s and a VMS or Unix license for it I find it
hard to imagine how Compaq could later claim the DS10 is not a supported
processor for VMS/Unix. It's certainly conceivable that some (far distant)
future version of VMS/Unix will stop supporting the DS10, but that's another
matter entirely - and is equally true for any current platform.

> 
> In addition the Digital UNIX license explicitly spells out what
> processors it may legally run on and the NT parts, including the P8
> are not included in this list.

    That's not the same issue. OS licenses are processor specific,
there's a different part number for each system (or group of "similar"
systems, such as PWSxxx). Since there was no part number for Unix on
the P8 systems there was no way to buy a Unix license for it and
purchasing a license for a different processor with the intent of
running it on the P8 would be a fradulent act. I certainly agree with
you there *should* have been a license available for systems like this,
but if Compaq chooses to not offer one there is no legal way to get one.

> 
> The bottom line is that it is not a good idea to cheat on processors
> for running Digital UNIX.  You will continue to see discussions on the
> net about how this should work but it is simply not a good business
> practice to follow.

     Agreed. But since the DS10 is licensable for both VMS and Unix this
should not be an issue with the current offering.

   I'd also like to note that much of the current discussion has focused
around whether hobbyist VMS will run on this system. The rules of the game
are different for the hobbyist license - you can pretty much try to run it
on anything you want ( eg there have been files released to get it to run
on the Multia, which was never a supported VMS platform ). 

