Erik Corry wrote:
> 

> For floating point the Alpha is ahead if I use Digital Fortran.
> Using egcs/gcc I don't think there is any difference.  In that
> case Athlon at 600 gives 17.5 SPECfp and the DS20 at 500 (with
> a much better motherboard) gives 22.8 (source c't magazine 18/99).
> If Compaq is dissatisfied with egcs they could pay someone
> to improve the Alpha port.  That's what Intel did.
>

g77 is pretty disappointing on the Alpha, but Compaq has ported
their compilers to Linux Alpha so there you go.  
 
> So for better floating point performance (if I recompile) I am
> paying twice the price and accepting a much lower selection of
> precompiled apps.  That may be an interesting proposition for
> some physicists, but it's not the mass market Alphas for NT
> and Linux we were promised.  No wonder noone was buying NT for
> Alphas.

Recompiling your code on different machines is a way of life for anyone
in computational chemistry.  When I buy boxes for computational work
I don't care if there is *any* precompiled software for them.  I just
install the OS, Fortran and C compilers and get to work.  There is a 
fairly sizable number of people in this business doing the same thing,
although I'll admit the market is orders of magnitude smaller than
the "I wanna play rad Quake dood !" crowd.

NT on the Alpha was crap to begin with, which is why it never really
took off.  Our NT Alpha never integrated quite right with the rest
of our Intel/NT cluster.  Also, the performance of the newer Alphas
(21264) absolutely sucked under NT.  I ran some standard benchmarks
under NT and could never reach the speed others were posting from
runs on Tru64 machines.

Jeff




