Heh.  I may not have been articulate in the past, but when this first came
up this was my first thought.  

Quite a good speculation, and one that probably plays part to the whole
sorry affair.  But Compaq is a bit schizophrenic at times, and there's
probably a Wintel, M$FT kiss-up and bedmates portion as well.

However, it is good to note that Compaq was one of the first major OEMs to
use non-Intel chips on some product lines, that they've always supported and
distributed non-M$FT OS's in their startup kits for their x86 servers (SCO,
OS/2 Warp, and Novell).  In short, we shouldn't ever completely sell Compaq
short down the x86, Wintel line...

And hey, imagine this portion of the scenario:  You're the CEO, you've just
acquired a large competitor who happens to have their own major software
products, both in terms of Operating System and other packages.  Up until
now, for the most part, you've just been a big box builder, selling lots of
hardware (and bits and pieces of software, but nothing at all on the level
of an OS). 

You start reviewing your new operations numbers, and notice the type of
thing Aaron surmises below.  To add another wrinkle, you see *lots* of this
going on, money flowing out from your two Operating Systems groups, as well
as from your partnership with another OS vendor.  The money seems to be
going to ISVs...

You, wise CEO, hardware guy, think "Wait-a-minute, why am I subsidizing
developers and subsidizing this other OS as well?  Don't people write
software for platforms because of profit potential and market share?"

BZZT!  That's one wrong for the CEO.  The truth of the matter is that many
big (and some small, depends on your niche) ISVs are paid for the
development efforts by vendors who have their own OS, often in the form of 
hardware, software, but sometimes even as investments or contract 
developers on your payroll working at the ISV.  This might seem odd to the 
average joe, or to somebody who's spent their time in the one portion of 
the market that this rule doesn't apply, M$FT-land.  Truth is, it is a bit 
bizarre, especially when you are funding somebody else's development directly, 
but it happens.  The traditional policy of buying platform loyalty is still 
around to some extent.  It can be argued that software and even hardware
support for ISVs is good, and even investments or the occasionally
development staff should be available, but it's definetely an odd practice. 
One can argue that Open Source is a vehicle for changing this, and if that's
the case, all the better.

Oh, and those of you in the know may derive certain opinions about my
comments with respect to the organization I work for and certain decisions
made by them at various times in the past, in a similar vein.  I'm not going 
to deny or confirm anything, I'm just a field Systems guy who won't admit to 
knowing anything :)  My opinions are definetely my own, and I mean no ill
will or poor view of my company to be portrayed.

Chris

