I don't know how much of what was said below is fact, and how much rumor.
But when you evaluate who got hurt by dropping NT on Alpha, it's hard to
argue that it was MS:

- they already have the benefit of all the 64-bit development on Win2K, so
it's as ready to port to IA64 as it will ever be;

- MS also already holds most of the technology it was interested in
obtaining from Compaq (especially given that MS development appears to
consider concurrent shared-disk applications uninteresting);

- if NT on Alpha sales were insignificant to Compaq, they were far more
insignificant to MS;

- running as a 32-bit OS on Alpha didn't give NT any magical superiority
(more addressable memory or higher-count SMP viability, for example), since
it was still effectively the same code ("bug-for-bug" compatible, reportedly
required by MS), so its suitability for 'enterprise' use enjoyed no real
advantage over a high-quality Intel platform;

whereas Compaq lost significant credibility with both its existing NT on
Alpha customers and the rest of its non-Wintel customer base by bailing out
so abruptly.

So if Compaq did indeed intentionally slap MS by dropping NT on Alpha, it
also in the process shot itself in the foot.  It's hard to see that as a
gutsy move, just a stupid one.

- bill

