You have to ask a hard question.  Ok, here's part of the answer.

Hardware: $3,595 (SCSI version) or whatever better price you can get.

Base VMS: In the past DEC was giving a credit (20% or 75%) for VAX
          licenses.  If you work at it, you can get some concessions.

	Base VMS for workgroup machines is (from faulty memory) $1,200
	or thereabouts.

	TCP/IP comes with the NAS license, or standalone, and is close
	to $3,000.  Don't need it, save the money.  Also, while I am using
	TCP/IP V5, I will pass on one tidbit.  A good friend of mine recently
	told me about routing DECnet IV over IP only network segments using
	Multinet at both ends.  He thinks TCPWare will also do this.  So,
	if you need to route DECnet over IP and do not like Phase V then
	get the Process product.  You will need it at both ends.

Users:	Here's where it gets interesting.  If they were tied to the
	HW, then see note on credits above.  If they were not tied
	to the hardware, then you can have one of several license
	types, but the one you want is (I forget part #) the one that
	is good for either VAX or Alpha.  Ok, QL-MT3AA-?? is the number
	you want.  If you have QL-XULAA-?? or QL-09SA9-?? then they
	are tied to VAX archetecture.  An important note, if the user
	licenses have a support contract, then you should be able to
	have the latter 2 license types converted to the QL-MT3AA type
	license as part of your support.  Anybody with the older types
	and a support contract should get the upgrade since the MT3
	licenses should work on any VMS system.

Disclamer, most of the above is from memory, and could be wrong, or
changed since I got my info.  I did pull up a S&O for the user licenses,
so they should be right.

Also, some proper groveling with your Compaq reps might get some better
pricing and/or upgrade of the software.

Dave

