Jean-Marc Lasgouttes (lasgoutt@fantomas.inria.fr) wrote:
: Now I am not so sure about that... As far as I know, PS/2 keyboards pinout
: is something like

: pin     assignment
: 1       KB Data
: 2       NC
: 3       Gnd
: 4       +5V
: 5       KB Clock
: 6       NC

: However, on both of our pws500, the pins that are connected are 2, 3, 4 and 6.

: What does this mean?

That you are not counting the pins right?

The DPW service guide gives the keyboard connector pinout as:

Pin Signal
 1   KBData
 2   N/C
 3   Ground
 4   +5 VDC
 5   KBClock
 6   N/C

(which is obviously the same as yours). Pin 1 is the *upper* pin on the
part of the connector bulging right towards that cute keyboard icon,
opposite the latch pin. The numbering goes see-saw fashion from there on, 
so that it looks (very rougthly) like:


             3  
         5       1
           ==
         6*      2*
             4

I certainly know that PS/2 keyboards work on DPWs, as I exchanged an el-cheapo
horror which came with my desktop clone for the very nice keyboard from a DPW, 
and both systems lived happily ever after ;-)

Cheers,

/Serge.P

