|> I have managed to resurrect an old VAXstation 3100. It contains
|> old source "repositories", i.e. simply some old versions of the
|> files. These are not really important, but anyway I want to get
|> them onto a Sun machine and put them into SCCS, RCS, CVS, ;-)

Zane H. Healy suggested to set up a Linux box with DECnet support.
Which Linux versions or distributions have DECnet support? On our
S.u.S.E. 5.3 box, I found only a file /usr/src/.../decnet/README
saying "this is being worked on". Grmpf.

Glenn Everhart, Robert Koehler, and Arne Vajhoej suggested to use
the CMU IP package for VMS. Alas, this is the classic chicken&egg
problem - if I had a means to get this package onto the VAX, I
probably wouldn't need it any more :-/ Anyway, I'll try to find
it, and get it onto a VAX tape the next time I visit the customer.

Tim Shoppa, David A Froble, Robert Koehler, and Arne Vajhoej
suggested to use a serial line and Kermit. See above :-/

Arne Vajhoej suggested to use tar/VMS to write a tape and read it
back on the Sun. See above :-/

Adam Price suggested to use a null-modem. Hmmmmm... Hmmmmm...
I have had much trouble with that VAX's serial ports in the past
when setting up a serial printer... but hey, what about capturing
the printer output! I think I'll try that. Set up a perl script
to listen on /dev/ttyb. The header line inserted by PRINT will
come in very convenient to sort out the files and recover their
dates.

Francois Pinard <pinard@IRO.UMontreal.CA> warned me that I might
encounter record blocking problems. Well, I have transferred the
recent versions of those files (development has been taking
place at customer's site for the last two years or so) using
some Windows ftp client and WinZip/unzip without problems. And
if I really decide to capture printer output, PRINT will take
care of it :-)

