"Big upgrade fee"?? "Linux only SRM"?? Are we sure? Because I can still
get all the firmware's I want from the appropriate Compaq web page:

	http://ftp.digital.com/pub/DEC/Alpha/firmware/

Lo and behold, the first on the list is the DS10. From it's page I can
download the AlphaBIOS or SRM firmware without even being asked for a
username. One would also think that if there was a "Linux only" SRM, it
would be listed on this page (or at least a note about it somewhere). At
least I didn't see one under the DS10's listings. That, and Linux can be
booted from either SRM or AlphaBIOS. I think the instructions on the
AlphaLinux web pages use AlphaBIOS (or they did the last time I
installed is on my AStation255/233).

Now there MIGHT be a few old DEC Alphas out their which are permanently
NT-crippled, and maybe some Alpha clones which are "Linux-crippled"
(http://www.thelinuxstore.com). Unless Compaq is no longer installing
FlashROM in their machines, I think something is misunderstood
somewhere. What we need is the official word from Compaq (which I
thought they gave long ago).

Thor Lancelot Simon wrote:

> DEC has recently shipped some boxes with "Linux only" SRM -- normal SRM,
> except that the systype reported to the operating system isn't one that
> Digital Unix will recognize and run on.  To run DU, they give you new SRM
> (for some boxes) if/when you pay a big upgrade fee.  For other boxes (e.g.
> Alphaserver 330x) there isn't any SRM that will run DU at all, even though
> the machines are totally normal examples of a systype that DU would, if it
> were reported correctly, run on, e.g. the AlphaServer 1200.  This is the SRM
> that's on some of the "white box" formerly NT-only Alphas that have been seen
> up for auction lately.  I don't know if VMS will run with this SRM or not,
> nor do I know if it's in fact what's on these budget "Linux Screams on..."
> DS10s, but since it runs Linux or NetBSD just fine, there's no way to be
> sure it isn't without ordering a box and trying to boot VMS or DU on it.
