In article <7ufi3b$9i0$1@mailint03.im.hou.compaq.com>, hoffman@xdelta.zko.dec.nospam (Hoff Hoffman) writes:
>    o if somebody did load SRM on one of these Windows NT only boxes, it 
>      probably reported a weird system identification code.  Hence some 
>      of the confusion that apparently arose here.

I think Compaq could earn a bit of goodwill if it put in an engineering
change so that all future OpenVMS/Tru64 releases do the following: 

 During boot it looks for an SRM variable ALT_SYSID.  If found, it uses 
 that ID, otherwise it checks for the regular system ID. If the ID it ends 
 up with corresponds to a supported machine ID, it would continue booting
 as if the underlying machine was actually of the type indicated. 

That would seem like a fairly trivial change to put into the OS (3 lines of 
code???) and would allow people to convert those NT specific machines which
can reasonably be converted.  That is, those which just need a different
card or two and SRM loaded.

Moreover, as knowledge accumulated about how to convert these machines
Compaq (or DECUS, or somebody) could put up a table which looks like:

Model 1:  replace scsi card with zyx, set alt_sysid 12345
Model 2:  replace scsi card with zyx, replace graphics card with supported
          model, set alt_sysid 12346
Model 3:  set alt_sysid 12345
etc.

I can even imagine situations where some hardware upgrade might convert a
*supported* machine from one type to another, and the ALT_SYSID would allow
the user to change the id of the machine without having to load a whole new
SRM. 

Sure, a user could abuse the ALT_SYSID to screw up a machine, for instance,
identify some little workstation as a huge server, but there would be 
little point to that since it probably wouldn't even run that way.  I guess
they might also be able to go the opposite way, identify a huge server as a
small workstation, and so be able to use cheaper licenses.  This is likely
to load the right bits at boot time only in those cases where the sole
difference between "workstation" and "server" is completely arbitrary, ie,
the XP900 vs. DS10, but that's yet another argument against the arbitrary
assignment of function/price to identical hardware (which is what the NT
specificity was all about too). 

Just a thought...

Regards,

David Mathog
mathog@seqaxp.bio.caltech.edu
Manager, sequence analysis facility, biology division, Caltech 



