In article <380C5BBE.B737ADDB@cs.vu.nl>,
Krzysztof Ostrowski  <kostrow@cs.vu.nl> wrote:
>This is exactly the problem. I have both the books from Amazon.com, including
>Pfister, but it is really not enough for someone who wants to go into the 
>things deeper and some more theoretically.

  Well, comp.arch is a good place to talk about it, though you're likely 
to get a fairly mixed dose of theory and practice here rather than just 
theory. You mentioned reliability .. what kind of reliability are you 
interested in?  Reliable RPC?  Transparent failover?

>By the way, have you (or anyone) heard about some theoretical research group?
>All one can find on the Web is progress in building more and more powerful 
>clusters, but hardly any general ideas.

  I personally haven't .. the heavy-hitting cluster tech researchers might 
be in the employ of private corporations, and so barred from publishing.
As you've noticed, there's a *lot* of business interest in clustering, as 
an underdeveloped technology with tremendous commercial potential (ie, for 
building faster, more reliable web servers, DNS servers, database servers, 
et al).  So I wouldn't be surprised if the best minds have been hired away 
from pure academia.

  Another newsgroup you should try is comp.sys.dec -- VMS is one of the 
best (if not the definitively best) general-purpose clustering operating 
systems ever made.  Folks savvy with VMS clustering technology are likely
to know why VMS clustering was implemented the way it was implemented, 
and their explanations should give insights to many issues in clustering
technology.  They might also know who in academia is researching cluster
computing and where to find published works on the topic.

  I've set followups in this article to include comp.sys.dec.

  -- TTK




