I know, I should probably let sleeping dogs lie, but I heard some
speculation the other day that makes too much sense to ignore.  And
this is speculation based on rumors, so take the whole thing with a big
grain of salt...  (and no, I've not been drinking)


Change your mindset for a minute.  Become the CEO of a big company.
You've got the greatest processor on the planet: 64 bits, fast, and
good.  You help to port an OS to the platform, you struggle with less
than marginal help (after all, your OS partner was forced into the
relationship for legal reasons).  The OS partner requires you to do all
the work -- the porting, the testing, the new development of new
technologies.  Then they bill you for it.

You bust your buns and come up with a better mousetrap.  You do things
the OS company can only dream about with THEIR operating system.  You
bring technology to fruition years ahead of the schedule that they've
got with their traditional legacy partner.

What does the OS company do?  Embrace it?  Market it?  Make money with
it?

No, they squish it.  They say "Thank you for doing the prototyping for
our legacy partner; we'll release the product when they're ready to
ship their chip."

Do you think you'd feel like showing them the New Jersey state bird?

Yes, but you do one better -- take away your developers, any way you
can.  Yank the new technologies.  Cut them off at the knees, take away
their development environment that you've labored so long and hard on
porting, testing, and getting out the door.



Back to reality.  Yes, I'm talking about Windows NT on the Alpha
platform.

I'm not just speculating on 64 bit NT here, although that was obviously
the proverbial straw that broke the camel's back.  Consider this list
of technologies:

 - VLM -- Alpha can address up to 28 GB with 3 year old technology,
   hundreds with what's about to hit the street.  NT can address up
   to 3 GB on Intel, 2 GB on Alpha.  Huh?

 - Clustering -- Digital Clusters for NT was released before Wolfpack,
   was better featured, and had a much better growth plan.  Digital
   gave it all to M$ and quit marketing the product.

 - Compilers -- GEM is a gem.  VC and VB and VFORTRAN all have the
   same code generator, just different front ends.  How much money
   was Digital paid for that?????????

 - FX!32 -- yeah, but who needs it?  Well, maybe not all of it, but
   Merced will definitely benefit from the thunking technologies that
   the FX!32 team perfected and built into the core of W2K.

 - Outlook -- an after hours port by a couple individualists was
   underway and nearly complete.  The MS counterpart was discovered,
   and was threatened with his job if he didn't cease with the
   project.  After hours support and access to the source code
   disappeared overnight.

 - Office 97 -- The Q asked to port Office 97; M$ said "sure, you can
   port it and support it, and we'll only charge you $5M (US)
   _PER_COMPONENT_" for a total of $25-30M...  PER YEAR.  And the
   Q would have to do all the work!

and one more...

 - Wildfire.  If you're not familiar with Wildfire yet, read up on it
at:

    http://www.digital.com/alphaserver/announce/oct99_preview.html

Digital and MS promised 32 processor NT systems last year.  Guess what
MS won't be able to deliver on?  Can you say  "high availability" and
"scalability to 32 or 64 or 128 or more processors."?

I hypothesize that Compaq pulled the plug now because it was tired of
spending millions to port NT, tired of developing new technologies like
VLM and 64 bit computing and clustering and not seeing them get into
THEIR products where they could have a competitive advantage, and tired
of being asked to give MS more money to have the honor of doing more
work for M$.

They pulled the plug before they gave some really cool technology to
MS.  They pulled it at a time when Wintel really needed it the most,
right when they were getting close to porting 64 bit NT to IA64, when
Microsoft is getting hammered for NOT having an enterprise solution.
And then they threw all their technical might behind the one OS that
stands the greatest chance of unseating MS, one which is already 64 bit
(thanks to Alpha) and has a good Alpha following: Linux on the desktop
and low-end farms, Tru64 for the high end enterprise (and on anything
BUT IA-64, as long as its name is spelled "A-l-p-h-a".)

If anyone inside the Q would care to lend some credence to this
speculation, I'm all ears and no lips.

Aaron
