Michael Friedlander wrote in message <380C984E.B51F6F7F@stanford.edu>...
>Our lab just inherited a couple of Digital Unix boxes.  We were just
>handed the units along with the root passwords and we have no disks or
>manuals.  When booting, the machines are looking for a now defunct NIS
>server, and the boot process seems that it will wait indefinitely for a
>response from that server.  If I hit cntrl-c, the machine will skip the
>search for the NIS server and will show me a boot prompt, but will hang
>when I try to log in as root.
>
>It seems that I need to boot into single user mode to disable NIS.  Is
>there a way of doing this without a boot floppy (e.g., something like
>the rescue disk that can be made under Linux)?
>
>Please cc any response to mpf@stanford.edu.  Thanks in advance for the
>help.
>
>--Michael
when the machine starts to boot, you will see a sequence of numbers across
the top of the screen, if you repeatedly press cntrl-c from this point, you
will eventually get to a prompt that looks like this
>>>
From here you can boot to single user mode using the command
boot -flags s

Hope this helps
Adam
PS Lots of good documentation at
http://www.unix.digital.com/faqs/publications/pub_page/
PPS if it is a server not a workstation then it probably has a halt
button which will take you straight to the chevron prompt.







