MS has been bailing significant functionality out of Windows 2000 for
some time now and it still won't be ready this year, it appears.  I still
have yet to hear anything about Windows 2000 that sounds new.  The
current marketing campaign seems almost like they are saying that
it's so revolutionary and new that they can't write about it, you have to
see it to believe it and you'll be completely blown away.  A lot of
organizations are holding their breath in anticipation.  IBM, Compaq
and a lot of others have significant complimentary campaigns gearing
up for the Windows 2000 release.

We'll see.  If it's a disappointment, if it fails to deliver on the #1
problem from the Enterprise perspective - stability - then Linux
may see even more dramatic growth than it is now.

I'm hopeful that Linux can put us in a new marketplace where
OpenVMS will get more looks.  Unix vendors, already working
long hours to incorporate the functionality and reliability of
OpenVMS Clusters into their offerings could be squeezed
seriously by the advent of Linux.

We're starting to hear that the Galaxy/Wildfire promises are to
some extent true.  We could see new OpenVMS systems
marketed by the end of the year that will provide many multiples
of performance over the current offerings in a single box.

E*Trade seems to have been fairly stable lately.  Northernlight
is a beacon for OpenVMS.

Sun is starting to get assailed from all sides.  The Linux people
don't trust their "Open" license, Java may be the most developed
and underused technology of all time, new offerings from IBM and
Compaq threaten Sun's Enterprise Unix market.

We could see intense competition among the traditional Unix
vendors as they attempt to deal with a loss of part of their
market to Linux boxes.  This could lead to uncertainty in
the Unix Enterprise marketplace.

In this new environment perhaps shops will start thinking
about Unix alternatives for new deployments.  Especially a
Unix alternative that is in use by so many conservative operations
today like Banks, Stock Markets, Telecomm companies...

One thing that Compaq might seriously consider is pushing
efforts to do a Linux Affinity project for OpenVMS like they
tried for Windows Affinity.  Get tools, software and packages
in place so that Linux and OpenVMS can work well together.
A lot of that's already in place, DECC is better at porting
Unix software than ever, the Apache server has a beta port,
Perl works well.  More could be done.

I don't believe that business is ready for Linux everwhere.
In this environment, there might be more places for something
like OpenVMS.

-Jordan Henderson
jordan@greenapple.com





