It's kinda hit and miss.  For DEC-branded graphics boards, usually
DUNIX/Tru64 has an excellent driver, and in many cases Linux has no
driver.  For generic hardware, in many cases Linux is your only
choice.

In my experience, Linux works better on small-memory systems.  You need
at least 64M and perferably 128M to run DUNIX/Tru64.

In my experience, one big factor in favor of selecting DUNIX/Tru64 is
that it comes with a stable version of Netscape.

