In article <7ufds2$4io$1@panix6.panix.com>, tls@panix.com (Thor Lancelot Simon) writes:

  There is confusion spanning across several issues here.

  Let's start with the easy part:

    There is no such thing as a Linux-only SRM console.

  And another easy part:

    The Linux AlphaServer DS10 boxes can be used by OpenVMS Hobbyists.

  Ok, set your "way back machine" for the domain of Microsoft Windows NT 
  on Alpha, and specifically on Alpha systems targeted for Windows NT...

  There are Alpha systems that were sold for Windows NT only, and these
  systems had system identification values that (officially) permitted 
  only Windows NT operations.  Given the way these systems and system 
  consoles were configured, it would not surprise me that:

    o these Windows NT Alpha systems had odd system identification codes.
      (That was, in fact, part of the core of the Windows NT restrictions
      implemented for these older Alpha systems.)

    o these Windows NT systems did NOT have supported SRM consoles.
      (OpenVMS workstation support was not -- at that time -- generally
      available nor -- unfortunately -- viewed as interesting, and there 
      were few if any new OpenVMS workstations around.  Decisions in this
      area have obviously been reversed, and OpenVMS workstations are now 
      available to customers, and are believed interesting to customers.)

    o if somebody did load SRM on one of these Windows NT only boxes, it 
      probably reported a weird system identification code.  Hence some 
      of the confusion that apparently arose here.

    o Linux didn't care about the system id, and booted anyway...

  All discussions of the console system identification values aside, the 
  DIGITAL Personal Workstation series Alpha systems had a number of subtle
  variations, and some of the "-a" series systems variations -- the Personal 
  Workstation "-a" series systems were designed and sold and intended for 
  Windows NT only -- contained hardware designs that would make OpenVMS 
  operations (and support) rather more difficult.  (AFAIK, these low-level 
  differences were not intentional, they were merely an outgrowth of the 
  Windows NT focus for the boxes, and the choices of integrated circuits 
  and I/O widgets accordingly made.)  In various cases, the only way to
  tell which system variant you had was to look at output from a console 
  command -- there was no particular external indication.

  I am not aware that these DIGITAL Personal Workstation "-a" series systems 
  were sold (by DIGITAL or Compaq) as Linux systems -- these "-a" series 
  boxes were regularly sold as Windows NT systems, however.  There were 
  also some DIGITAL Server boxes -- largely all variants on the AlphaServer 
  4100 series platform and derivatives -- that were sold for use (only)
  with Windows NT, as well.

  The AlphaServer DS10 and (AFAIK) all current Alpha system designs do not 
  have any console mechanisms that would preclude operation of any operating 
  system.  With the appropriate software licenses, OpenVMS or Tru64 UNIX 
  can be booted on these systems.

  Again:

    There is no such thing as a Linux-only SRM console.  (A system with
    an AlphaBIOS console restricted to Windows NT, yes...)

  Further:

    All recent Alpha system hardware designs are NOT platform-restricted.

  And in conclusion:

   The AlphaServer DS10 does NOT contain a Linux-only (or NT-only) console.


 --------------------------- pure personal opinion ---------------------------
   Hoff (Stephen) Hoffman   OpenVMS Engineering   hoffman#xdelta.zko.dec.com




