In article <xLjL3.1547$Jp4.1507@news20.bellglobal.com>, "Neil Rieck" <n.rieck@sympatico.ca> writes:
:As I currently understand UTC (Coordinated Universal Time), in an ideal
:world all machines (networks, satellites, computers and their file systems,
:etc.) would be set to the same world clock. Computers would not change their
:clocks when moving to or from daylight savings time. Programs displaying
:time to a local user would read a local flag and then present the internal
:time (or file "time stamp") in an external local format.

  That's the theory.  In reality, timezones and such are still controlled
  by rather fickle governmental organizations -- some geographies switch, 
  some do not, some switch in units of an hour, and some do not.  Simply
  tracking the daylight savings time switch-overs around the world is an 
  "entertaining" project.  Some businesses can and do switch, and some 
  cannot or do not.  Some businesses have systems and clusters that operate 
  across timezones, and some do not.

  Further, OpenVMS has an extensive base of system and application software 
  that contain embedded quadword format time values.  These "native" quadword
  values are manipulated and stored all over the place.  It is simply not 
  feasible to change this.  (eg: We would need extensive modifications to 
  the existing file structure, and to related file system routines and 
  interfaces.  When/if/should we release a new file structure for OpenVMS, 
  I would tend to assume that it will have integrated UTC capabilities.)

:Most UNIX systems currently do not need their clocks changed twice a year
:but the latest version of OpenVMS (7.2) still does. Does anyone know whether
:Compaq has and plans to build this feature into OpenVMS?

  We will NOT be automatically changing the system time.  We MAY be adding 
  a way -- beyond the use of the SYS$EXAMPLES:DAYLIGHT_SAVINGS.COM command 
  procedure -- to allow an automatic changeover to be selectively enabled
  by the system manager.  We ARE working to simplify the timezone support, 
  and on migrating certain applications -- such as DECnet-Plus -- into the 
  use of the underlying OpenVMS UTC services.  (I can't say when -- or even 
  if these -- and other related changes will be released.)

:p.s. I'm aware that there is limited time zone support built into VMS 7.2
:but apparently this only supports the "C" run-time library.

  UTC timezone support first appeared in OpenVMS in early V6.  C moved to 
  the OpenVMS services, as part of the on-going work to centralize and to
  simplify this "quagmire".

  Applications that use the existing UTC services or C calls are "ready" 
  now.

 --------------------------- pure personal opinion ---------------------------
   Hoff (Stephen) Hoffman   OpenVMS Engineering   hoffman#xdelta.zko.dec.com




