This is a follow up to my post yesterday. (UTC and OpenVMS 7.2)

After a bit more digging I've come up with the following UTC related
details.

1. If you want to see Compaq's official (but foggy) view of UTC, then check
out the online system manager's manual at URL
http://www.openvms.digital.com:8000/72final/6017/6017pro_016.html#set_time_s
ec

2. My current employer requires me to use VAX-BASIC. Since that language had
limited internal TIME STAMP support I always call the following two OpenVMS
system services from within VAX-BASIC:

SYS$ASCTIM
SYS$BINTIM

Compaq has added 2 new alternate system services that incorporate UTC into
their conversions called SYS$ASCUTC and SYS$BINUTC. (there are also many
others like SYS$BINUTC, SYS$GETUTC, SYS$NUMUTC, SYS$TIMCON etc.) For more
info start checking from URL
http://www.openvms.digital.com:8000/72final/4527/4527pro_003.html#index_x_56

3. Compaq refers to a program called SYS$EXAMPLES:DAYLIGHT_SAVINGS.COM which
should be run twice a year to adjust programs that require these changes
(specifically, any programs that call date + time functions in C-RTL and any
POSIX compliant programs). They also refer the system manager to the
following files in SYS$MANAGER which are:

UTC$CONFIGURE_TDF.COM
UTC$TIME_SETUP.COM
UTC$TIMEZONE_SETUP.COM

4. I can't find any UTC or TIME ZONE support in DCL's lexical library
(specifically F$CVTIME and F$TIME). Compaq may not wish to open the door to
the possibility of queuing batch jobs with a UTC based time stamp. However,
I can think of a couple of reasons why you might wish to do this. NOTE: this
might be one the reasons why UNIX seems ahead of OpenVMS in the UTC area;
cron jobs just aren't as robust as the batch processing found in OpenVMS.


I'm still left with my original question which is "does anybody know whether
Compaq has any plans to build more UTC related time into OpenVMS?" (I'm
specifically thinking about displaying the TIME ZONE in directory listings
etc.)

Neil Rieck
Kitchener/Waterloo/Cambridge,
Ontario, Canada.
