Hi,

Carlyle F. Smith <imardmd1@aol.comQunspam> wrote:
: Hi, folks -- DEC CPUs are new to me, and I would really appreciate
: some helpful comments/links.

: I have a VAXStation 3100, DECStations 2100,  3100, 5000/120,
: and 5000/125.

Hmmm, a rather nice collection ...

: The connections for hooking a display and keyboard
: to the VS3100 and DSx110s are real clear. But neither of the DS5000s
: have any obvious place for console I/O.

: Q1: I get the idea that the DS5000s need graphics adapters to plug
: into one of the white bus risers on the systemboard (the hole
: coverplate behind the riser is missing -- looks like a card has
: been removed). Where could I get one real cheap? 

Seems like the graphics card is missing (btw., the DECstation bus
system is called TurboChannel). You should be able to find one 
failry cheap (try misc.forsale.computers.workstations). Low-level
cards are called PMAG-BA or PMAGB-B.

: Q2: How does the kbd attach? I see a DB-15 male connector on
: the back with a kbd outline above it, but ????

: Q3: How does a mouse attach? To the kbd? To the display?
: There doesn't seem to be any typical RJ-11 series mouse port ---

You need an adapter cable from DB-15 to keyboard (RJ-11) and mouse (9-pin
Mini-DIN). Alternatively you can simply connect a dumb serial terminal
(9600 baud 8N1) to serial port #3.

: Q4: One of the DS5000s has a RRD42-AA CD-ROM, but no
: caddy for it. Any sugestions? I have caddies for NEC or Toshiba
: SCSI CD-ROMs, and one for an early Philips/Laser Magnetic
: external drive, as well.

The RRD42-AA is a modified Toshiba XM3401, IIRC.

: Q5: Can't I attach just any SCSI external CD-ROM to the
: DECStations for software loading?

You need a CD-ROM that can be set to 512 byte block length (e.g. a Teac
532S, most modern Plextors or an old Toshiba XM3401). Depending on the
firmware, I have at least one machine that refuses to boot from anything
but an orignial DEC CD-ROM drive.

: Could I link the VS3100
: (which has a 3.5" floppy) for minor data/file I/O?

If the VS3100 runs Ultrix or VMS with some sort of TCP/IP stack, you can
transfer files via ftp.

: I also have some Storage units, one with a TZ30 tape drive.
: One has two RZ55E (320Mb) drives, and another has two 
: RZ57E (1Gb) drives. A couple more  have no drives, just the
: box. I suppose I can just populate and stack these, as 
: data storage for the CPUs, eh?

Yep.

