Neil Rieck wrote:
> First off, I am not a big fan of UNIX but my current job brings me into
> daily contact with at the system manager level. I deal with Sun Solaris,
> HP-UX, and of course OpenVMS. The two UNIX OSs that I just mentioned do not
> need their system clocks changed twice a year but  it seems that OpenVMS 7.2
> does (and I was posting to this news group to see if anyone else had crossed
> this path in the OpenVMS world).

A VMS system can change DST itself, but th efunctionality is build into
DECnet phase V/OSI/Plus.

Not particular logical and VMS engineering has also stated elsewhere in
this
thread that they are considering moving the functionality to base VMS,
where
it belongs more logical.

The functionality is there, but just in a starnge place.

> When I execute the UNIX equivalents of "SHOW SYS", "SHO TIME", and "DIR" (I
> won't pollute this newsgroup with the laughable UNIX blasphemous
> equivalents) the time stamps are displayed based upon a user defined
> preference (if defined) or the default for the machine that he is logged
> into. You always see the date and time along with the time zone. This is a
> big deal if you've two people logged into your system from various parts of
> the world. (I realize this situation doesn't affect most but it does happen
> and the world is getting smaller). I suspect the reason that the reason UNIX
> has got this working is that all their apps are written in "C" using date
> and time functions that support UTC.

What you want is not UTC support (which VMS have), but support for
concurrent users in different time-zones (which VMS does not have).

The latter do need UTC or something equivalent, but it needs a lot
more than that.

> I don't think it would be a big deal to do the same in OpenVMS

Normal VMS times is 8 bytes, while VMS UTC times is 16 bytes.

Making the general use of UTC quite a big change.

> So we're still left with my original (but rephrased) question; "Does anyone
> know whether Compaq has any plans to move the OpenVMS clock closer to the
> UNIX model?" (or are they just waiting until after the Y2K barrier has
> passed by?)

I would phrase the questions: Will Compaq change VMS to support
time-zone
support on a per-process basis.

Arne



