I am aware of a feature which is built into TCPware's NTP which will allow
the clock to drift to the point where you want it. But this is NOT what goes
on in popular UNIX systems when they go in to, and out of, daylight savings.
For example; in DigitalUNIX 4.2 the system clock does not change but the
user's display does. This is what I think we need in OpenVMS.

My ultimate vision goes something like this: Every machine, every network,
every computer, every satellite, everything that has a clock must be sync'ed
to UTC (a variation of GMT). Every machine will know what rules are required
for displaying the local time to humans. Every machine will know the local
rules and make the change automatically. If a guy from Germany is logged
into the same machine as a guy from California, they will each (if they so
desire) see time stamps on files that represent their own local times
because the file time stamp will be recorded as UTC but what each user will
see is what ever his local time is set to. There will be NO changing of the
computer clock, just a shift in what each human sees.

p.s. Sorry if this has come across as a little head strong but I feel this
thread is beginning to take on religious proportions.

