Here is some additional info:

The following is from the 5500 manual, and is practically word for word.

CPU Cover Panel

The panel has a two postion Function switch on the left side of the panel,
and a three positon Operation switch on the left side, both these switches 
being just below the LED display and just above the MMJ.

Function switch - When the switch is in the down position (dot outside the
circle), breaks are disabled.  When the switch is up (dot inside the circle),
breaks are enabled.  When breaks are enabled, pressing the Break key on the
console terminal halts the processor and transfer control to the console
program.

The setting of the Function switch is also used in conjunction with the
three position Operation switch to control what occurs during a power-up
sequence.


Operation switch - This three position rotary switch determines how
the system responds at power-up.  The Operation switch and its uses
relative to the Function switch setting ar as follows:

Action Mode (up - face) - If the Function switch is set to down, the
system displays a Language Selection Menu at power-up.  If your console
does not support multiple langauges, it defaults to English.  After you
select a langauge, the power-up tests are run, and the console prompt (>>)
is displayed.  If the Function switch is set to up, the console serail line
external loopback test is executed.  This function requires special
loopback connectors and is for Digital Field Service use only.

Normal Mode (arrow) - Power-up self-tests run, and the system either enters
the Normal console moed, indicated by the >> prompt, or the system 
autoboots from a device previously defined by the user.  The system
autoboots only if you have set the bootmode environment variable to a,
and have assigned a value to to the bootpath environment variable.
In this mode the Function switch enables/disables breaks as described above.

Maintenance Mode (circle T) - Power-up self-tests fun.  Then the system
enters Maintenance mode, indicated by the >>> prompt.

----- end of manual contents -----

Lay translation - Put the Function switch in the down position and the
Operation switch in the middle position and fire it up.

----- from the manual -----

Red LED display

The diagnostics run automatically on power-up.  While the diagostics are
running, the LED on the CPU cover panel displays a hexadecimal countdown
of the tests from F to 4 (though not in precise reverse order) before
booting the operating system, and 2 to 0 while booting the operating
system.  A different countdown appears on the console terminal screen.

----- end of manual contents -----

Normal power up is to count down through the self-tests and display the
>> prompt or autoboot is so configured.

There is no list of what all these self-tests numbers mean in the manual
that I have for the 5500.  All it states is that if the self-tests halts
due to an error, to copy the number following the question mark and
'call your Digital service representative'.  Yeah, right :-(

Falling back to experience on other DEC machines, such as a DECstation 3100,
I have seen the self-test complain if the Ethernet is not terminated, and
you may need to terminate the SCSI bus as well.  You seem to have 3 problems
during the self-test, with any luck it might get of one or two of them.
Won't hurt to try.

Mike




