In article <7t04cv$90v$1@nnrp1.deja.com>, dlee20@my-deja.com writes:
:I need a port testor for vax terminals or schematics or plans for
:building my own port testor.  I just need to be able to test if the
:network port is live or not.  we have many problems with dead terminals
:but sometimes the port is dead and in production critical situations a
:testor would really help alot.  We use VT320,420,510 & 520 terminals.

  What specifically are you looking to test?

  VT terminals do not connect directly to a traditional network, they
  connect to a host system or to a terminal server.  The easiest way
  to detect a failed terminal server is to plug in a known-good serial
  terminal device -- either another terminal, or a palmtop or portable,
  or a loopback, or any of the more sophisticated (and expensive) serial 
  line testing widgets that are around.

  As for testing the terminal's serial port, the easiest approach is a 
  "loopback".  Effectively, a serial line loopback connects the transmit 
  and the receive signals together via some wiring added to the back 
  side of a standard connector.  Most every VT-series terminal was 
  shipped with a loopback, and these are generally available.  (The
  OpenVMS FAQ has pinouts for the MMJ.  The EIA232 DB25 pinout is
  available at various spots around the 'net.

  To use a loopback, simply plug it into the terminal's serial line
  connection.  Everything you type should now be visible as you type 
  it.

  Most folks working in this area use a palmtop or portable with testing 
  software or with basic serial communications capabilities, and a simple 
  device known as a "breakout box".  The former looks at the serial 
  communications traffic, while the latter examines the signals and 
  electrical connectivity.  Having a couple of loopbacks is also very
  convenient, but the palmtop serial line can also be used to test out 
  the serial line...

:If anybody can help please e-mail me at donald.lee@navistar.com

  Ask here, get an answer here.

