> I had the same problem. The only way to get the tape out was a little
> tricky. The load/unload mechanism is driven by a small motor and can't
> be operated by hand. It gets the power via a small plug from the circuit
> board. Removing this plug and applying a current from an external low
> voltage supply (3-5V) ejects the tape.

You could try what one guy did to get a floppy out of his floppy disk
drive (He didn't know about the eject button). Take a stick of butter
and melt it down. Pour this into your drive, then grab onto the tape
with a large pair of pliers and yank it out (ignoring any
broken bits of plastic in the meanwhile). Then call the supplier and
complain about their broken equipment.

You may also want to try to hold down the eject button while turning
on the drive. This works on some models, but I'm not sure about the
tlz04.
(but, it also isn't as much fun as the butter option)



