FYI, a new version of E11 Demo is available for download from www.dbit.com.
The improvements over V2.1 are mainly bug fixes, plus a few new features:

- CDROMx: disk device allows reading PDP-11 CDs using MSCDEX.EXE (etc.) and
  any CD-ROM drive.  Note that the emulation of MSCDEX in the W9x DOS box is
  broken, it refuses to read the first 16 sectors of the disk so it's useless
  with PDP-11 CDs, real DOS is fine though.

- Command files can be nested, SET/SHOW BREAK allows specifying an ASCII
  character that pops up the prompt in case you've got the console reASSIGNed
  to a serial connection where it's not easy to generate a BREAK (reverse LAT
  or whatever), and there are a couple of other new things too.

Commercial customers will receive update disks by mail as usual.  This time
around they were written by E11 itself -- in case anyone else is weird enough
to want to drive a diskette duplicating machine from a PDP-11, my utility
is available here:

	ftp://ftp.dbit.com/pub/pdp11/rt11/dupe.mac

It runs stand-alone (but can be started from RT-11) and needs enough PDP-11
memory to hold a copy of the disk, so E11 Demo can't run it, but it ought
to work on a real PDP-11 (Q- or Unibus, it uses the Unibus map if one is
present) with an aftermarket MSCP controller that supports whatever kind of
floppy drive the duplicator has.

Also, a Linux port of E11 is now well under way, but unfortunately there
probably won't be a freeby Demo version of this one.  Figuring out how to
load and run a segmented assembly language program under Linux was hard
enough, I don't want to think about how much worse it would be to run half
of the program in V86 mode too!

John Wilson
D Bit



