  Did you previously request a layered product license? Since the info
on the VMS license is unique to the license (system ID), the generator
makes one each time you ask for a new system, while it remembers when
you first asked for layered product licenses and hands you the same ones
if you ask again. At least that's the way it appears to work.

> "normally" the printer port. (Another clue? The license doesn't
> install properly from the bootup script, I have to do it manually
> using LICENSE after the script finishes...) 

  I seem to recall that was a documented restriction at one time, but I
don't remember if that was during the beta of 7.2 or the official release.

> OK. So now I want to install Pascal. (Stop snickering.) First,
> I register the pascal license (which is accepted). Then, using 
> vmsinstal, I install pascal056. I accept the default responses
> including YES to running IVP after installation. (clue? The
> license lists version as 0.0?)

  How did you register the license? With the @sys$update:vmslicense command
or by typing in the license register command you got in your hobbyist email?
If the latter, you need to do a "license load pascal" (or reboot) to load
the license into memory.

  There are a bunch of other things that are less likely (a pre-existing
expired/incompatible Pascal license, for example) but let's work on the
easy ones first.

  A 0 in a field just means "not used" - so a version of 0.0 means "allow
any version".

